The Bullet Cluster (1E 0657-56) Does Not Require Dark Matter

Abstract

The X-ray and gravitational lensing observations of the Bullet Cluster (1E 0657-56) are frequently cited as the strongest evidence for the existence of dark matter. In this paper, within the framework of Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe (TDMU 2.33), we propose an alternative interpretation that requires no introduction of unknown particles. In this framework, matter is treated as topological knots in a combinatorial network, nuclear transformations are attributed to defect-complementary weaving (Path I) and forced chain reconnection (Path II), gravity is regarded as a statistical effect of the total number of shared links, and light is viewed as the propagation of orientational perturbations of shared links. We demonstrate that the observed mass–gas spatial separation, high-temperature X-ray emission, iron abundance characteristics, and the optical darkness of foreground objects can all be naturally derived from known mechanisms—namely, compact remnants (topological stars, woven solidification states), core–envelope separation of camouflaged giant stars, and instantaneous nucleosynthesis via the leapfrog path. Several testable predictions that distinguish this framework from the standard ΛCDM model are presented.

Keywords: Bullet Cluster; dark matter; Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe; leapfrog path; compact remnants

1. Introduction

The Bullet Cluster (1E 0657-56) formed from the collision of two galaxy clusters and is located approximately 3.8 billion light-years away. High-temperature gas observed by the Chandra X-ray Observatory (shown in pink) and the mass distribution reconstructed from gravitational lensing (shown in blue) exhibit a significant spatial offset. This is commonly interpreted as ordinary matter decelerating due to electromagnetic interactions, while dark matter passes through collisionlessly. This interpretation has become one of the most widely cited pieces of observational evidence for the existence of dark matter.

However, decades of searches in terrestrial laboratories have failed to detect any dark matter particle candidate. This compels us to reexamine the Bullet Cluster phenomenon and investigate whether a complete explanation can be constructed without invoking unknown particles.

Based on the axioms of TDMU 2.33 [1], this paper presents a purely geometric interpretation. This interpretation assumes no new particles beyond the known constituents of the Standard Model, nor does it invoke singularities from general relativity or the standard model of accretion disks. All physical quantities involved are reduced to combinatorial operations on nodes, directed edges, shared links, and reconnection counts.

2. Theoretical Foundation

This section lists only the TDMU concepts directly cited in this paper; the complete axiomatic system can be found in [1].

2.1 Topological Knots and Matter

Matter consists of stable loop-woven subgraphs on the membrane, termed topological knots. Nuclides correspond to stable topological knots on the recursive growth tree, uniquely labeled by the following geometric quantities:

N_i: the total number of primitive bricks constituting the topological knot

A_def(i): the surface defect area fraction, defined as the ratio of unwoven surface nodes to total surface nodes

E_resist(i): the compressive resistance binding energy tensor, composed of the core primitive brick number, shell port saturation, and suture line stress concentration factor

2.2 Path I (Defect-Complementary Weaving)

When the surface defects of two topological knots are geometrically complementary, they can interlock to form a larger topological knot, releasing stress waves. The event rate is:

R = N_A · N_B · P_match

where P_match is determined by the defect areas and the hierarchical truncation criterion. Path I does not depend on energy barriers; it depends solely on the geometric matching of defects.

2.3 Path II (Forced Chain Reconnection)

When the external compressive energy exceeds the E_resist of a topological knot, the connecting edges are sheared in bulk, triggering forced chain reconnection.

2.4 Energy Conversion and Extremely Slow Dissipation

In the superfluid state (D_ij Θ = 0), the stress energy released by Path I and Path II is predominantly converted into vortex twist energy (Writhe × Twist) via a three-step mechanism. This mechanism significantly suppresses outward transverse wave (photon) radiation.

However, topological mismatch stress between the superfluid boundary and the underlying woven network produces an extremely low-rate background longitudinal wave leakage (δρ_link mode). This leakage is suppressed to an extremely low level by phase locking, and its products are ultra-low-temperature stress waves rather than normal light. Thus, a topological star is not an absolutely closed eternal entity, but a quasi-steady-state system with an extremely long dissipation timescale—its behavior resembles a high-speed, smoothly spinning gyroscope: macroscopically manifesting as near-freezing of energy, yet with an irreversible, exceedingly faint unwinding process occurring at the底层. This dissipation mechanism ensures the system satisfies the second law of thermodynamics.

2.5 Light–Gravity Decoupling

Gravity is carried by the total number of shared links (N_total), while light is carried by orientational perturbations (δA). The two are determined by completely independent geometric degrees of freedom.

2.6 Leapfrog Path

Owing to the hierarchical truncation criterion ([2]), topological knots of any mass number can undergo aggregation without being restricted to adjacent masses. Cross-mass-number aggregation channels, which are typically neglected in the standard model, are automatically included in TDMU by geometric construction.

3. Observational Facts of the Bullet Cluster

The following are the three raw observational results, unmediated by theoretical interpretation:

Gravitational lensing mass distribution (blue): Weak gravitational lensing reconstruction shows that the majority of the total mass is concentrated at the collision front and approximately coincides with the positions of visible-light stars.

X-ray emission (pink): The high-temperature (approximately 100 million K) plasma emission region lies behind the mass region and exhibits a bullet-shaped morphology. X-ray spectra show characteristic emission lines of highly ionized iron (Fe XXV), oxygen, silicon, and other elements.

Optical/near-infrared images: A large number of stars are visible in the foreground region, but corresponding gas and dust emission is absent. In个别 foreground regions, compact star clusters corresponding to rear X-ray cavities have been identified.

4. TDMU Interpretation

4.1 Origin of the Mass–Gas Separation

In TDMU, gravitational lensing traces the total number of shared links (N_total), a quantity primarily contributed by the inertia of compact topological knots. The gravitational contribution of gas (atomic nuclei and electron clouds) constitutes a relatively small fraction of the total mass.

Stars and topological star remnants (including the exposed bare cores of camouflaged giant stars) have extremely small volumes (radii far less than 1 AU). During a galaxy collision, their effective collision cross-section with the tenuous medium approaches zero, so they experience almost no deceleration, retaining their original velocity (approximately 4500 km/s) as they pass through, forming the foreground mass region.

Ordinary gas has an enormous volume, and the shared-link density gradient produces strong ram pressure drag, leading to deceleration and accumulation, forming the rear X-ray region.

This separation is not determined by particle properties, but by the geometric difference in spatial extent.

4.2 Origin of the X-ray Emission

The rear X-rays originate not from nuclear fusion or accretion friction, but from the dramatic shared-link density gradient changes experienced by a vast number of topological knots (atomic nuclei) at the shock front when the two gas clouds collide. Macroscopic kinetic energy is first converted into longitudinal waves (phonons/thermal motion), and at the density gradient region of the front, part of the mode is converted into transverse waves (photons), i.e., the thermal bremsstrahlung continuum. Simultaneously, shock compression elevates the Path I event rate, and defect-complementary weaving of light nuclei (hydrogen, helium, etc.) releases additional stress waves, jointly sustaining the high temperature of approximately 100 million K.

4.3 Geometric Interpretation of Iron Abundance

The observed iron characteristic line abundance is relatively high. The standard model requires extended stellar nucleosynthesis or supernova ejection to accumulate iron.

The TDMU leapfrog channel ([2] Theorem 3) provides an instantaneous synthesis path: under the high-pressure environment of the shock, intermediate-mass nuclei can rapidly generate iron-group elements through leapfrog aggregation such as ²⁸Si + ²⁸Si → ⁵⁶Ni. Because ⁵⁶Fe resides in a perfect closure configuration (A_def → 0, ℐ = 0), its outflow channels (continued aggregation or fission) are geometrically suppressed, allowing its abundance to accumulate. Thus, the iron abundance can be regarded as a product of the collision event itself, without requiring an extended history of stellar evolution.

4.4 The Darkness of Foreground Objects

Camouflaged giant stars ([3]) possess a "core topological star + external supersaturated envelope" structure. Under the ram pressure of the collision, the external envelope is stripped away, becoming part of the rear gas.

The exposed bare core (topological star) is in a phase-locked superfluid state. The energy released by internal Path I and Path II is overwhelmingly converted into vortex twist energy and contributes to the effective mass, producing no detectable transverse wave photons. As described in Section 2.4, the bare core is not "zero dissipation"; rather, at the boundary layer, there exists an ultra-low-frequency longitudinal wave leakage driven by topological mismatch. The products of this leakage are ultra-low-temperature stress waves (phenomenologically corresponding to an ultra-low-frequency background disturbance), whose energy per single event lies far below the sensitivity threshold of all current electromagnetic-band detectors.

For the residual accretion disk, because the particle number density N plummets, the Path I event rate R = N_A · N_B · P_match is mathematically always greater than zero, but its radiative power (proportional to N²) decays with the square of the density, likewise falling to unobservable levels.

Therefore, the optical darkness of bare cores is the result of a dual suppression—extremely slow dissipation and extremely weak coupling—manifesting observationally as complete electromagnetic silence. Their extremely long dissipation lifetimes (far exceeding the Hubble time) enable them to become the dominant dark mass carriers in galaxies: remnants left behind after stars expire continue accumulating to this day in a gyroscope-like, low-consumption mode.

5. Comparison with the Standard Model

The standard ΛCDM model requires the introduction of cold dark matter particles (such as WIMPs) to explain the gravitational lensing mass; it requires the assumption of stellar historical nucleosynthesis to explain the iron abundance; and it requires the assumption of accretion disk inflow to explain active phenomena.

The framework presented in this paper eliminates these exogenous assumptions at the foundational axiom level. All phenomena are attributed to the same set of combinatorial rules: mass–gas separation arises from geometric differences in spatial extent; iron abundance arises from instantaneous synthesis via the leapfrog path; and foreground darkness arises from the dual suppression of extremely slow dissipation and rarefaction.

6. Testable Predictions

Based on TDMU, the Bullet Cluster and similar collisional systems should satisfy the following:

Microlensing event rate: The foreground blue region contains a large number of compact remnants (with a continuous mass distribution). The microlensing optical depth is approximately 0.04, with event timescales correlated with remnant mass (averaging about 10 years)—testable by the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope survey.

X-ray iron lines and the leapfrog path: The spatial distribution of iron should coincide with the shock front, rather than with the stellar distribution. In low-metallicity environments, the ⁵⁸Ni/⁵⁶Fe ratio is predicted to vary with environmental density, distinct from the smooth spectrum of sequential nucleosynthesis models.

Photon capture shell thickness: If VLBI imaging of foreground compact objects is performed, a photon ring of finite thickness (ΔR_ph > 0) should be measured, as opposed to the zero-thickness photon sphere predicted by general relativity.

Merger gravitational wave echoes: If two topological stars merge, gravitational wave echoes with a discrete spectrum should subsequently appear, corresponding to the reorganization modes of the vortex network, distinct from the purely damped signal of the no-hair theorem.

Compact remnant accumulation effect: A large number of low-inertia compact remnants should exist in galactic halos, their total mass contribution should be sufficient to explain the characteristics of galactic rotation curves, and the mass spectrum should exhibit a peak near approximately 1.2 M_⊙ (determined by the optimal configuration of recursive packing).

7. Discussion and Open Questions

The interpretation presented in this paper relies on certain mathematical quantities within TDMU that have not yet been analytically resolved, including:

The precise value of the hierarchical truncation constant κ and its functional relationship with the local loop density

The complete classification of geometric matching indicators for the leapfrog channel

The precise mapping between the compressive resistance tensor and nuclear mass

The quantitative value of the critical mass for superfluid shell closure

These open questions are listed in Section 10 of [1]. In the specific case of the Bullet Cluster phenomenon, the qualitative conclusions of this paper—that the three major observational facts can be explained without dark matter particles—do not depend on the precise values of these parameters.

8. Conclusion

The multi-band observations of the Bullet Cluster can be self-consistently interpreted within the TDMU framework without the need to introduce dark matter particles.

Mass–gas separation: Arises from the geometric difference in spatial extent between compact objects (stars + topological star cores) and fluffy gas.

X-rays and iron abundance: Produced by shock-driven Path I reactions and instantaneous synthesis channels of the leapfrog path.

Foreground darkness: Arises from the extremely slow dissipation (gyroscope-like operation) of bare cores and the precipitous drop in radiative power of the residual disk surface.

This interpretation is not in conflict with existing observational data and yields predictions that are clearly distinguishable from the ΛCDM model. The Bullet Cluster should not be regarded as proof of the existence of dark matter, but should be re-examined as a dynamical case of core–envelope separation occurring in compact remnants and gas during a collision.

